# Part A — Game Design Document

## 1. Game Summary

The Driftwood Diner is a **2D side-view** cosy mystery cooking game set on a fog-covered island. The player washes ashore with no memory of how they got there and no way to leave. They take shelter in an abandoned restaurant, get the lights on, and start cooking. Drawn by the smell of food and the glow of light, islanders begin to arrive. Through repeated visits and shared meals, the player earns the trust of the island's residents, uncovers the history of the restaurant, and must ultimately decide: save enough to pay for passage and leave, or choose to stay.

The port is controlled by soldiers — passage off the island costs money ($10,000). By cooking meals and serving island residents, the player earns income and trust, uncovers the island's history through NPC stories and corkboard items, and faces their choice. If the player earns $12,000 without choosing to leave, the game ends with them building a life on the island.

## 2. Setting

A small unnamed fog-covered island. The diner is the only playable space, rendered as a **2D side-view cross-section** — the player sees the interior as if looking through a cutaway wall, like a dollhouse. The wider world is implied — glimpsed through large restaurant windows (parallax background layers showing the harbour, fog, distant coastline, lighthouse) or described in NPC dialogue, photographs, and corkboard notes. The military presence at the port is a known, visible fact of island life; NPCs reference it openly. It is not a twist — it is the reason leaving costs anything at all.

The diner begins overgrown and abandoned. Over time it becomes warmer and more lived-in, reflecting the player's progress. This environmental progression happens automatically at story milestones.

**Implied locations (seen through windows or described in dialogue):**
- Rocky coastline and fog-covered beach
- Distant lighthouse
- Dense treeline inland
- Disused harbour and dock (where the military checkpoint sits)
- Remains of a small village

## 3. Core Loop

### Day Phase
The diner is closed. Quiet and unhurried. No time pressure. The player can move around the space, cook, examine the corkboard, review discovered recipes, check savings, and prepare for the evening. Warm daylight streams through the windows (parallax background shifts to daytime).

### Night Phase
The diner opens. NPCs arrive on their own schedule. The player cooks using the combination system and serves dishes. Serving the right dish deepens relationships, earns payment, and advances story arcs. The counter persists between nights — items left out remain until used or they go stale. The night phase ends when the last NPC leaves. Window parallax shifts to night; interior lighting becomes warm and cosy.

### Weather
Fog and rain change daily (simple random roll). Weather affects which NPCs appear. Rain is visible through windows and as particles; fog reduces parallax background visibility. Advancing the day triggers overnight recipe completions and stale item checks.

## 4. Mechanics

### 4.1 Discovery Cooking (Combination System)

The core mechanic. When the player opens the cooking UI (C key), a **2D overlay** appears showing a virtual counter surface. The player drags ingredient icons from a pantry panel onto the counter and combines two items to attempt a recipe. Successful combinations produce a dish or a new ingredient. Failed combinations produce nothing. There is no recipe book shown in-world — **discovery is the reward**.

- ~30 base ingredients available as icons in a pantry panel within the UI
- **120+ combinations** including chained recipes (A+B=C, then C+D=E)
- Some recipes require leaving items on the counter **overnight** before completing
- Items left out for **4+ nights** go stale
- A **recipe log** panel tracks all discoveries; undiscovered recipes show as ???
- Three log views: **Discovered** (found so far), **All** (full list with ??? for unknowns), **Overnight** (recipes requiring resting)
- Badges indicate chained recipes and overnight transforms

### 4.2 NPC Visits

NPCs arrive at the diner during the night phase on their own schedule. The player does not control who arrives or when. NPCs walk in from the entrance (side-scrolling entry), navigate to a seat using simple 2D pathfinding, sit down, and wait. The player cooks dishes in the UI and serves them.

- Serving a **positive dish** (matches affinity) increases relationship meter, earns payment, triggers new dialogue
- Serving a **neutral dish** yields smaller payment, no relationship change
- **Ignoring** an NPC across multiple nights causes their relationship meter to **degrade** — if it hits zero they stop visiting (soft fail)
- Some NPCs pay more generously than others; some reward with **rare ingredients** instead of money
- Some NPCs have **conditional triggers** (fog, rain, story milestones)
- NPCs occasionally leave items on the counter when they arrive or depart
- Leaving specific items on the counter **triggers NPC comments** on arrival — a small detail that makes the world feel alive

### 4.3 Dialogue System

**No dialogue choices.** The player listens. NPCs have tiered dialogue states that unlock as relationship meters fill through cooking and repeated visits. Early dialogue is guarded and brief. Later dialogue is personal, warm, and story-revealing. Dialogue is displayed in a dialogue box UI.

### 4.4 Corkboard

A physical corkboard mounted on the diner wall. The game's **primary lore and world-building system**. NPCs occasionally leave behind 2D illustrated items on the counter — photographs, hand-drawn maps, torn notes, newspaper clippings, receipts. The player picks these up (E key) and pins them to the corkboard.

The corkboard is a dedicated interactable zone — when the player stands near it and presses R, the camera zooms into a **full-screen corkboard UI** where items can be examined up close. The corkboard fills gradually over the course of the game, telling the island's story without cutscenes. Some corkboard items unlock late-game NPC dialogue or affect ending conditions.

### 4.5 Environmental Progression

The diner visually improves at story milestones **automatically**. No player action required — these changes happen as a reward for relationship progress.
- **Early game:** dark, dusty, overgrown. Minimal furniture, dim lighting.
- **Mid game:** warmer lighting, cleaner surfaces, small details appear (a plant, a photo on the wall).
- **Late game:** genuinely warm and inhabited. The diner feels like home.

Implementation: 3 visual tile/decoration layer swaps at story milestones via `diner_progression.gd`.

### 4.6 Recipe Log (UI)

An in-game UI panel tracking all combinations the player has discovered. Visible on demand (Tab key).
- **Discovered** — what you've found so far
- **All** — full list with undiscovered shown as ???
- **Overnight** — recipes requiring overnight resting
- Badges indicate chained recipes and overnight transforms

## 5. Player Controls

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| **A/D** | Walk left/right within the diner |
| **W/S** | Climb ladders or stairs (if diner has loft/second level) |
| **E** | Interact with nearby objects (pick up items, examine objects) |
| **C** | Open/close the cooking UI overlay |
| **Tab** | Open/close the recipe log |
| **R** | Examine corkboard item up close (zoomed UI view, requires proximity) |
| **Esc** | Close any open UI |
| **Mouse** | Point-and-click for UI elements and nearby world objects |

Interaction is proximity-based via an **Area2D** around the player sprite. Interactable objects show a subtle outline or icon prompt when in range.

## 6. NPC Roster (~17–20 Total)

### Deep Arc NPCs (2–3) — Full multi-stage story. Tied to the restaurant's history. Requires specific dishes and many visits to fully unlock.

#### The Washed-Up Traveller
Arrived on the island years ago, just like the player. Knows every corner of the island but deflects questions about why they never left. Becomes the player's confidant over many visits. Their arc reveals the truth about what keeps people on the island and recontextualises both endings. **The player's clearest mirror.**

- **Affinity:** comfort food, simple hearty dishes
- **Schedule:** appears most nights, one of the first regular visitors
- **Leaves:** corkboard items about the island's geography, old maps
- **Arc depth:** 8–10 tiers of dialogue, unlocked across many nights
- **Story role:** final arc reveals why the island holds people; reframes the leaving/staying choice

#### The Elderly Baker
Her bakery closed years ago. She knew the restaurant's original owner personally. Guarded and nostalgic at first. Only specific **baked dishes** earn her trust. Unlocking her full arc reveals the most about the restaurant's past — who ran it before the player, why it closed, and what happened.

- **Affinity:** baked goods exclusively (bread, pastries, cakes)
- **Schedule:** every 2–3 nights, regular
- **Leaves:** old photographs, a pressed flower (key story item), recipes from the original menu
- **Arc depth:** 6–8 tiers, requires specific baked dishes at key moments
- **Story role:** Pressed Flower chain → Memory Cake → full ending dialogue unlock

#### Third Deep NPC
*To be designed mid-development.* Possible directions: the lighthouse keeper who sees things in the fog, or the harbourmaster who enforces the military's port rules but has doubts.

---

### Medium Arc NPCs (8–10) — 3–5 visit arc. Personal story, emotionally resonant.

#### The Failing Fisherman
Goes out every morning, returns empty-handed. Gruff and dismissive at first. Over time reveals he is **not really fishing for fish** — he's looking for something he lost at sea.

- **Affinity:** seafood dishes (ironic — he can't catch any himself)
- **Schedule:** most nights, arrives early and leaves early
- **Leaves:** a hand-drawn map of the coastline, fishing lure (ingredient)

#### The Newcomer
Recently arrived on the island, also confused. Bonds with the player over being outsiders. Friendly and open from the start. Their reason for being on the island turns out to be **more deliberate than it first appears**.

- **Affinity:** anything — not picky, grateful for any food
- **Schedule:** every night for the first week, then intermittent
- **Leaves:** a torn letter (corkboard item), unfamiliar spices (ingredient)

#### The Musician
Missing their instrument. Hums while eating. Quiet and melancholy. Their arc concludes with them **playing music again** — the diner gains **ambient music as a permanent reward** (replaces the night ambient track).

- **Affinity:** sweet dishes, desserts
- **Schedule:** every 2–3 nights
- **Leaves:** sheet music (corkboard item)
- **Reward:** unlocks the musician track (permanent night ambient upgrade)

#### The Storm Visitor
**Only appears during fog or rain.** Says very little. Leaves a corkboard item each visit without explanation. Other NPCs **do not seem to know who they are**. The most mysterious NPC — their identity is never fully resolved.

- **Affinity:** warm drinks, soups
- **Schedule:** fog/rain nights only
- **Leaves:** one corkboard item per visit (cryptic notes, old photographs from before the island changed)

#### Additional Medium NPCs (4–6, designed during development)
Candidates:
- **The Lighthouse Keeper** — maintains the light, sees ships that never dock
- **The Local Historian** — knows the island's official history, but not the unofficial one
- **The Drifting Merchant** — trades rare ingredients, always has a story from elsewhere
- **The Retired Sailor** — stopped going to sea but won't say why
- **The Doctor** — tends to the island's small population, worried about someone specific
- **The Teacher** — runs the island's tiny school, curious about the player's past

---

### Light Arc NPCs (6–8) — Recurring background characters. Dialogue warms over time but no full arc. Makes the diner feel inhabited.

- **The Quiet Farmer** — few words, big appetite, tips well
- **The Chatty Harbour Worker** — gossips about port activity, mentions military movements
- **The Strange Child** — asks oddly specific questions about the restaurant's history
- **The Elderly Couple** — sit in the corner, say very little, share a single dish
- **The Soup Regular** — young person who only ever orders soup, seems to be avoiding something
- **The Night Shift Guard** — military, off-duty, surprisingly kind, conflicted about the port fees

## 7. Story Structure

### Act 1 — Arrival
The player washes ashore. They find the diner, get the lights on, and cook their first meal. The first NPC arrives (the Newcomer or the Traveller). The island feels vast, quiet, and strange. The military presence at the port is established early.

### Act 2 — Belonging
The diner fills with regulars. Fragments of the island's history emerge through NPC stories and corkboard items. The Washed-Up Traveller becomes a confidant. The Baker begins to open up. The restaurant slowly comes back to life — environmental progression kicks in. Something significant happened here, and the island does not easily let people go.

### Act 3 — Truth
The full history of the restaurant is revealed through the Baker's final arc and the last corkboard items. The player learns who ran it before them, why it closed, and what happened to the island. The Traveller's reason for staying becomes clear. The player faces a choice.

### Ending A — Leave
The player accumulates $10,000 and chooses to pay the port fee. The diner is left behind. Warm but bittersweet. A departure sequence plays — the player walks to the harbour, boards a boat, looks back at the diner's light one last time.

### Ending B — Stay
The player reaches $10,000 and declines to leave — or earns $12,000 without ever choosing to go. The final scene mirrors the opening of the Washed-Up Traveller's arc: one day, another stranger will wash ashore and find the lights already on.

**Neither ending is framed as correct.**

## 8. Win and Lose Conditions

| Condition | Type | How to reach it |
|-----------|------|-----------------|
| Ending A — Leave | Win | Accumulate $10,000 and choose to pay the port fee |
| Ending B — Stay | Win | Reach $10,000 and decline, or reach $12,000 (auto-trigger) |
| NPC departure | Soft fail | Ignore an NPC across multiple nights until relationship meter hits zero |
| Soft fail recovery | Recovery | Leave a specific dish on the counter overnight as an offering — takes several days, not guaranteed |

If a **deep arc NPC** departs permanently, certain story content becomes inaccessible for that playthrough. Both endings remain reachable but one may be harder.

## 9. Art Direction

**Style:** 2D pixel art, side-view cross-section. Warm, detailed, atmospheric. Inspired by the cosy melancholy of Dredge and the cross-section aesthetic of Terraria.

**Interior:** warm amber candlelight, hanging Edison bulbs, ivy creeping along dark wooden walls. Heavy vignette darkening screen corners. The diner is the warm heart of the game.

**Exterior (through windows only):** cold slate and teal, thick coastal fog, faint lighthouse glow. Rocky coastline. Never fully explored — always just out of reach.

**The contrast between warm interior and cold foggy exterior carries most of the atmosphere.**

**NPCs:** unique pixel art sprites per character. Readable silhouettes, expressive animations. Each NPC should be immediately identifiable by shape and colour palette alone.

**2D flat assets (corkboard items):** illustrated or hand-drawn style. Photographs, notes, maps, clippings, receipts. These are the game's primary storytelling medium alongside dialogue.

